11244	{"artifactId":"HLD-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330","entry":{"id":"review-c3ea2268118ef033528fc997e9ae7a61","actor":{"id":"insrc:review","kind":"system","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-30T19:38:24.961Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"warn","summary":"review: warn","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"design.epic","stage":"design.epic","verdict":"warn","findings":[{"claimId":"coverage-of-intent.1","ref":"coverage-of-intent","item":"coverage-of-intent","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Every DEF story (s1/s2/s3) and its acceptance criteria are designed into an HLD story boundary (6.1/6.2/6.3), and DEF non-goals (reviving the rolled-back investigation/qualification engine, building a new session-threading framework, replacing design-artifact review, inventing a new artifact type) are explicitly excluded in the HLD text.","evidence":"DEF-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330.json body.stories s1(ac1-ac6)/s2(ac1-ac4)/s3(ac1-ac4) and body.nonGoals map 1:1 onto HLD §6.1 (packet/checkpoint/first review), §6.2 (resume + re-review history), §6.3 (concurrency + cleanup); HLD §2 and §8 backward-compat sections repeat each DEF non-goal verbatim ('No ... multi-round dimension/chunk engine', 'Do not build a new thread subsystem', 'Design-artifact review is unchanged', 'Reuse the CR identity ... not a second resumable execution ledger').","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.1","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The existing CR validator (`validateArtifact` in runner.ts) requires the persisted `dimensions` array to match an expected dimension list exactly in count and order, and the legacy judge loop runs serially — so a new task-based body cannot be pushed through it unchanged, as [[c11]]/[[c12]] and the architecture section claim.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/runner.ts:364-373 `if (b.dimensions.length !== expectedDims.length) return ...` and the per-index `dimension[${i}]` check; the judge loop (runner.ts:228-244) awaits each dimension slot serially before the next. [files: src/workflow/code-review/runner.ts, src/workflow/code-review/types.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.2","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Existing code-review author routing is exactly as described: the reviewer party is derived from the Story's BUILD record author (not a caller-supplied string), with controller-authored code going to the daemon and daemon-authored code going to the controller loop.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/author.ts:22-29 `buildAuthorParty` reads `artifactJsonPath(...buildArtifactId...)` and returns `authorPartyOf(rec.meta)`; src/daemon/steps/code-review-step/handler.ts:13-17 documents and src/daemon/code-review-rpc.ts:125 enforces the same-party refusal. [files: src/workflow/code-review/author.ts, src/daemon/steps/code-review-step/handler.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"current-behaviour.3","ref":"current-behaviour","item":"current-behaviour","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Both providers already expose a `runReviewSession` entrypoint usable for a reviewer session ([[c6]]/[[c7]]), and AGENTS.md's 'no Promise.all to an LLM provider, always serial for...of' convention cited as a constraint ([[c9]]) is real.","evidence":"src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts:456 `async runReviewSession<T>(...)`; src/agent/providers/ollama.ts:712 `async runReviewSession<T>(...)`; AGENTS.md:122 'Never `Promise.all` anything that reaches an LLM provider ... always serial `for...of`'. [files: src/agent/providers/cli-provider.ts, src/agent/providers/ollama.ts, AGENTS.md]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.1","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The single checkpoint path `<project>/.insrc/tmp/code-review/<review-id>.json` follows an established `.insrc/tmp/<id>/...` scratch-directory convention already used elsewhere, rather than inventing an unprecedented storage location, and the atomic-replace write pattern it proposes mirrors an existing reuse candidate (the artifact store's atomic write/rename), consistent with the HLD's 'reuse existing provider/task/artifact infrastructure' framing.","evidence":"src/daemon/handler-runtime.ts:264 'Tmp directory at ~/.insrc/tmp/<sessionId>/'; src/workflow/artifact-core/store.ts implements the renameSync-based atomic-write pattern reused across the artifact machinery (grep hit alongside runner.ts's own atomic write). [files: src/daemon/handler-runtime.ts, src/workflow/artifact-core/store.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"new-versus-reuse.2","ref":"new-versus-reuse","item":"new-versus-reuse","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The HLD's claimed 'actual session-thread facility' that execution should integrate with does not yet exist in this repository (it is explicitly described as a separate, not-yet-merged branch), and the HLD does not wrongly claim it is already available to reuse.","evidence":"grep for 'session-thread'/'SessionThread'/'threadLifecycle' across src/ returned no matches, so the facility is not present in this codebase; the HLD itself hedges this correctly ('Integrate ... when available; do not ... assume another branch is merged'), so the design does not misstate current reuse, but whether that branch genuinely exists/will land as described cannot be checked from this repo alone.","action":"Confirm with the user/stakeholder that the referenced session-thread branch exists and its integration contract before S3 design.story work begins.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"could-not-verify"},{"claimId":"change-sites.1","ref":"change-sites","item":"change-sites","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The two existing production call sites of `runCodeReview` (the daemon's `codeReview.run` in src/daemon/code-review-rpc.ts and the controller's code-review-step handler) are the complete set of places the new task-based coordinator must be wired into, matching the HLD's architecture description of 'existing code-review entrypoint' on both routes.","evidence":"Grep for `runCodeReview(` / `codeReview.run` shows exactly two non-test production callers: src/daemon/code-review-rpc.ts:175-183 and src/daemon/steps/code-review-step/handler.ts (via controller.ts), plus the already-existing 'planned/standalone/bugfix' review-subject variants in src/workflow/code-review/subject.ts and subject-paths.ts that the HLD's acceptance-evidence paragraph says it must also exercise. [files: src/daemon/code-review-rpc.ts, src/daemon/steps/code-review-step/handler.ts, src/workflow/code-review/subject-paths.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"data-compatibility.1","ref":"data-compatibility","item":"data-compatibility","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Today's `CodeReviewMeta.schemaVersion` is pinned at the single constant `CODE_REVIEW_SCHEMA_VERSION = 1` with no existing version-discriminated read path, so introducing a schema-version-2 task-based body with explicit version-aware reader/writer logic (as S1 proposes) is genuinely new work, not something already handled, and is correctly scoped as new rather than misdescribed as already-reusable.","evidence":"src/workflow/code-review/types.ts:280 `export const CODE_REVIEW_SCHEMA_VERSION = 1;` with no branching on alternate versions anywhere in CodeReviewBody/CodeReviewMeta; the HLD §5.3/§8-A correctly states 'Add version-2 task-based CR reading/writing and validation alongside version-1 compatibility' as new Story-S1 work. [files: src/workflow/code-review/types.ts]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"boundaries.1","ref":"boundaries","item":"boundaries","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"The HLD does not redesign anything owned by the design-artifact review path (`insrc_review_step` / `review-step/handler.ts`) or by the separately tracked, rolled-back investigation epic (E20261028c32aa605) referenced in [[c3]]; it only touches the code-review (`insrc_code_review_step`) surface.","evidence":"HLD §2/§8 state 'Design-artifact review is unchanged' and 'Coding and build-validation changes from that analysis remain outside this epic'; no HLD section references `review-step`, `DEF/HLD/LLD` review validation, or reopens the rolled-back epic's artifacts beyond citing its ROLLBACK.md as a non-goal rationale ([[c3]] in DEF). [files: DEF-e6ddcdcb3d9cf330.json]","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"error-paths.1","ref":"error-paths","item":"error-paths","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each stated failure mode (absent checkpoint on resume/submission, wrong-reviewer caller, a second active start for the same review-id, a malformed/conflicting packet, and a persistence failure) is paired with a named detectable condition and a stated handling behavior, per the Request-error-handling section and the sc2 `ReviewError` union.","evidence":"HLD 'Request-error handling' paragraph enumerates missing-checkpoint / wrong-reviewer / malformed-packet-rejection / persistence-failed-retains-last-valid-file, each tied to a cause; sc2's `ReviewError.code` union ('already-running' | 'invalid-packet' | 'attempt-conflict' | 'missing-checkpoint' | 'wrong-reviewer' | 'persistence-failed') matches these one-for-one, and 'The daemon admits one active execution per project/review-id before returning acknowledgement' (Architecture §3) states the already-running detection point.","action":"none","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"},{"claimId":"tests.1","ref":"tests","item":"tests","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"Each DEF acceptance criterion across s1/s2/s3 has a corresponding tested-behavior statement in the HLD (either the §3 code-review-policy 'Acceptance evidence' fixture list for s1's review-substance ACs, or the explicit test-intent language in §6.2/§6.3 for resume/re-review/concurrency/cleanup ACs such as 'Daemon disconnect survival and controller disconnect/retrigger are distinct tested cases').","evidence":"HLD §3 'Acceptance evidence' paragraph lists fixtures for implementation-evidence/coverage/convention/quality/naming claims (s1 ac3/ac6-adjacent); §6.2 commits to testing resume/re-review precedence; §6.3 commits to testing duplicate-id rejection, concurrent different-ids, disconnect survival/retrigger, and cleanup-on-approval-only — each DEF ac has a named corresponding test intent, with exact test-case enumeration left to the downstream LLD/Plan stage as is normal for an HLD.","action":"At design.story (LLD) time for s1-s3, require each DEF acceptance criterion to be mapped to a specific named test case, since the HLD only states test intent narratively.","fixability":"manual","outcome":"holds"}],"counts":{"high":0,"med":0,"low":11,"unverified":1},"template":"design-spec","reviewedAt":"2026-10-30T19:38:24.961Z","model":"cli-claude:claude-sonnet-5","reviewedBy":"daemon"},"resolutions":{}}}}}
